      <p>The pattern is familiar to anyone who has been handed the keys to a running building: the pumps are turning, the valves are modulating, the BMS shows no alarms — and the building still will not cool properly. In the four real cases below, all documented by the engineers who diagnosed them, the equipment turned out to be fine. What failed was balancing, setpoints, or installation. Each case yields a first check you can run before anyone orders replacement parts.</p>

      <h2>1. The valve that forgot how to modulate — Field Museum, Chicago</h2>

      <p>As the Field Museum consolidated more air handling units onto its central chilled water plant over twenty years, the system drifted out of balance. During a later investigation, engineers found the two-way modulating valves on the coils hydraulically closest to the distribution pumps behaving as on/off devices: they cracked open, the near-pump pressure immediately overflowed the coils, and the valves ran pinned at 100 percent, unable to hold discharge air temperature.</p>

      <p><strong>The worked number.</strong> A modulating valve only controls flow when it is the dominant resistance in its branch — that is valve authority, <strong>N = ΔP_valve ÷ (ΔP_valve + ΔP_circuit)</strong>. Take a valve with 1.5 psi at full open in a branch whose circuit (excluding the valve) drops 13.5 psi: N = 1.5/15 = 0.10. At that authority, moving the valve from 50 to 100 percent open barely changes the flow — so the controller, seeing its setpoint unmet, drives the valve fully open. The valve looks broken. It is installed in a branch where no valve could modulate.</p>

      <p><strong>First check:</strong> when a valve hunts or pins at 100 percent, ask whether it is the branch closest to the pump. The fix in Chicago was rebalancing — which immediately restored modulation and flow to the remote coils — with pressure-independent control valves planned as the durable answer: a PICV holds its flow setpoint across the pressure swings that defeat a conventionally piped valve.</p>

      <h2>2. The valves installed backward — a 302-room hotel</h2>

      <p>A new hotel building with 350 fan-coil units and six air handling units could not be balanced: chilled water pumps ran near maximum while many distal rooms were starved of flow. The commissioning team's forensic step was to disassemble one of the automatic flow-limiting valves. Inside, two installation defects: a manufacturer batch with the flow-direction sticker printed the wrong way — valves installed backward — and rubber diaphragms that are position-sensitive; installed backward or missing, the valve opens with the flow and stops limiting at all.</p>

      <p><strong>The worked number.</strong> A flow limiter only limits once the pressure across it is inside its operating range — the project's TAB target was a minimum 2.0 ΔP at the worst-case unit's FLV. Below that floor, the "flow limiter" is just a fixed orifice: it neither limits nor protects, and the loop short-circuits through whichever valves fail open. That is exactly what the pumps were compensating for at nameplate amperage.</p>

      <p><strong>First check:</strong> before blaming the pump or the pipe sizing, pull one valve from the suspect batch and open it up — against the manufacturer's IOM, not the sticker. A batch defect is invisible from outside; it costs one valve teardown to find.</p>

      <h2>3. The setpoints nobody audited — a telecom facility in Illinois</h2>

      <p>A 350,000 sq ft telecom building called its design team back one year after a chiller plant upgrade. The symptom list: the chiller tripped on low flow several times a week; the plant saw a 3°F delta-T (low delta-T syndrome); space humidity sat at 62–65 percent; and pump VFDs put in bypass overheated and tripped. The audit found three wrong numbers doing all that damage:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>The chiller's flow-protection DP switch was set to full design flow, <strong>189 gpm</strong>, when the manufacturer's specified minimum was <strong>100 gpm</strong> — every flow condition between the two looked like a fault.</li>
        <li>The bypass flowmeter was uncalibrated and held its valve 100 percent open, pressurising the system and depressing the delta-T.</li>
        <li>Pumps were controlled to hold <strong>18 psi</strong> across the most remote AHU coil's pressure-independent valve — against a design requirement of <strong>5 psi</strong> — unnecessarily pressurising the loop and wasting pump energy.</li>
      </ul>

      <p><strong>The worked number.</strong> 18 psi where 5 psi is required is 3.6 times the differential the coil actually needs — pump head spent pressing water through valves for no heat-transfer benefit, with the bypass dumping it back to the chiller at 3°F. The verified fixes: DP switch reset to the manufacturer minimum (100 gpm), pumps reset to 5 psi at the remote coil, flowmeter calibrated and bypass held at 100 gpm. With those three changes the plant ran on one pump, and the VFD in bypass never rode low enough on its curve to trip.</p>

      <p><strong>First check:</strong> audit every protection and control setpoint against the manufacturer's minimum — not the design nominal. Design flow is a sizing number; the manufacturer's minimum flow is the number the chiller's protection actually needs.</p>

      <h2>4. The coil that rained — moisture carryover</h2>

      <p>The mechanism is simple hydraulics: air moving across a wet cooling coil shears condensate off the fin edges, and above a velocity threshold it carries droplets downstream — wet ducts, damp ceiling tiles, elevated supply humidity. The reference calculation: 10,000 CFM through a 20 ft² coil face equals 500 fpm. Standard coil-selection practice keeps cooling-coil face velocity below published carryover limits — under about 550 fpm, with 500 fpm a common specification ceiling.</p>

      <p><strong>The worked number.</strong> <strong>Face velocity = airflow ÷ coil face area.</strong> The formula runs in both directions: given the airflow you can check the coil you have; given a velocity ceiling you can size the coil face you need (10,000 CFM ÷ 500 fpm = 20 ft² of face area). A coil that carries over is telling you one of those two numbers is wrong.</p>

      <p><strong>First check:</strong> calculate face velocity from the fan's actual CFM and the coil's published face dimensions before condemning the drain pan or the trap.</p>

      <h2>The pattern</h2>

